var e={level:`B1`,type:`word-bank`,questions:[{id:`b1-wb-001`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'should' to give advice.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means something is forbidden. Seeing a doctor is not forbidden: it is good advice.`,can:`'can' expresses ability or permission, not advice.`,would:`'would' is used for imaginary situations, not for giving advice like this.`},bank:[`should`,`mustn't`,`can`,`would`],text:`You ___ see a doctor. You look ill.`,answers:[`should`]},{id:`b1-wb-002`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Because' introduces a reason.`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast. Here the rain is the reason for staying at home, so we need 'because'.`,so:`'so' introduces a result: 'It was raining, so I stayed at home'. Here the rain is the reason, so we use 'because'.`,but:`'but' adds a contrast. The rain is the reason for staying at home, not a contrast.`},bank:[`although`,`because`,`so`,`but`],text:`I stayed at home ___ it was raining.`,answers:[`because`]},{id:`b1-wb-003`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'do' homework and 'make' a cake. Example: 'I did the shopping and made dinner.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:made":`We don't 'make' homework. With homework we use 'do'.`,"0:took":`'took my homework' means carried it somewhere, not completed it.`,"0:had":`'had my homework' means you owned it, not that you completed it.`,"1:did":`With food like a cake, we use 'make' to talk about preparing it.`,"1:took":`'took a cake' means carried it away. The sentence is about preparing a gift.`,"1:had":`'had a cake' means ate it, which is strange as a birthday present for your mum.`},bank:[`did`,`made`,`took`,`had`],text:`I ___ my homework and then I ___ a cake for my mum's birthday.`,answers:[`did`,`made`]},{id:`b1-wb-004`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Fill in' means write information in the spaces on a form. Example: 'Please fill in your name and address.'`,wrongReasons:{"fill up":`'fill up' means make a container full, for example a glass or a car with petrol.`,"find out":`'find out' means discover information, not write it on paper.`,"give up":`'give up' means stop trying. You need to complete the form.`},bank:[`find out`,`fill up`,`give up`,`fill in`],text:`Can you ___ this form and give it back to me, please?`,answers:[`fill in`]},{id:`b1-wb-005`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A 'salary' is the money you get for your job. Example: 'Teachers want a higher salary.'`,wrongReasons:{price:`A 'price' is how much you pay for something in a shop.`,cost:`'cost' is the money needed to buy or do something, not money you earn.`,fee:`A 'fee' is money you pay for a service, like a lawyer or a school.`},bank:[`price`,`salary`,`cost`,`fee`],text:`He works very long hours, but his ___ is quite low.`,answers:[`salary`]},{id:`b1-wb-006`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb for a real future possibility. Example: 'If I have time, I will visit you.'`,wrongReasons:{"would go":`'would go' is for imaginary situations with a past verb in the 'if' part, but here we have 'is'.`,went:`'went' is past. Saturday is in the future.`,going:`'going' needs 'are' before it and is not a full verb on its own.`},bank:[`going`,`would go`,`went`,`will go`],text:`If the weather is nice on Saturday, we ___ to the beach.`,answers:[`will go`]},{id:`b1-wb-007`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple for things that are always true. Example: 'If you drop glass, it breaks.'`,wrongReasons:{melted:`'melted' is past. Facts that are always true use the present simple.`,melting:`'melting' needs 'is' before it. 'it melting' is not a complete verb.`,melt:`With 'it' the present simple needs -s: 'it melts', not 'it melt'.`},bank:[`melts`,`melting`,`melted`,`melt`],text:`If you heat ice, it ___.`,answers:[`melts`]},{id:`b1-wb-008`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form. Example: 'I'm looking forward to seeing you.'`,wrongReasons:{meet:`'to' here is a preposition, not part of an infinitive, so 'to meet' is wrong. Use 'meeting'.`,"to meet":`'looking forward to to meet' repeats 'to' and uses the wrong form.`,met:`'met' is past. After the preposition 'to' we need the -ing form.`},bank:[`met`,`meet`,`meeting`,`to meet`],text:`I'm really looking forward to ___ my cousins from Canada.`,answers:[`meeting`]},{id:`b1-wb-009`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'Would you mind' we use the -ing form. Example: 'Would you mind waiting a moment?'`,wrongReasons:{"to close":`'mind' is followed by the -ing form, not by 'to' + verb.`,close:`After 'mind' the base form is not correct.`,closed:`'closed' is past or an adjective. A polite request with 'mind' needs '-ing'.`},bank:[`to close`,`closed`,`closing`,`close`],text:`Would you mind ___ the door? It's cold in here.`,answers:[`closing`]},{id:`b1-wb-010`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'although' + a clause to show a contrast in the same sentence. Example: 'Although it was late, we kept working.'`,wrongReasons:{because:`'because' gives a reason. Being tired is not the reason she finished her homework.`,so:`'so' introduces a result and comes before the second clause, not at the start of the first one.`,however:`'however' cannot join two clauses in one sentence. It starts a new sentence and is followed by a comma.`},bank:[`because`,`so`,`although`,`however`],text:`___ she was very tired, she finished all her homework.`,answers:[`although`]},{id:`b1-wb-011`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'so' to introduce a result. Example: 'I was hungry, so I made a sandwich.'`,wrongReasons:{because:`'because' introduces a reason. Going to the park is the result of the closed museum, not the reason for it.`,although:`'although' shows a surprising contrast. Going somewhere else is a normal result here, not a contrast.`,unless:`'unless' means 'if not'. It is used for conditions, not to link a past fact with its result.`},bank:[`because`,`unless`,`so`,`although`],text:`The museum was closed, ___ we went to the park instead.`,answers:[`so`]},{id:`b1-wb-012`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional question: What will you do if + present simple? Example: 'What will you say if he asks?'`,wrongReasons:{did:`'did' is past. 'If the shop is closed' talks about a possible future situation.`,are:`'are' is not used with the base verb 'do'. 'What are you do' is wrong.`,have:`'have' would need a past participle ('done'), and the present perfect does not fit here.`},bank:[`will`,`are`,`did`,`have`],text:`What ___ you do if the shop is closed?`,answers:[`will`]},{id:`b1-wb-013`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We can use the -ing form as the subject of a sentence. Example: 'Walking is good exercise.'`,wrongReasons:{eat:`The base form cannot be the subject of a sentence here. Use 'Eating'.`,ate:`'ate' is past simple and cannot be the subject of 'is'.`,eaten:`'eaten' is a past participle. It needs 'have' or 'be' and cannot be the subject.`},bank:[`ate`,`eat`,`eaten`,`eating`],text:`___ too much sugar is bad for your teeth.`,answers:[`eating`]},{id:`b1-wb-014`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'when' after words for time, like 'day' or 'year'. Example: 'I remember the year when I started school.'`,wrongReasons:{where:`'where' is for places, but 'the day' is a time.`,who:`'who' is for people, but 'the day' is a time.`,what:`'what' cannot be used after a noun like 'the day'.`},bank:[`when`,`where`,`who`,`what`],text:`I still remember the day ___ we first met.`,answers:[`when`]},{id:`b1-wb-015`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Pick up' means collect someone, often by car. Example: 'Can you pick me up from the station?'`,wrongReasons:{"give up":`'give up' means stop doing something. You don't stop your sister.`,"take off":`'take off' is for clothes or planes, not for collecting a person.`,"run out":`'run out' means have no more of something. It does not mean collect.`},bank:[`pick up`,`take off`,`run out`,`give up`],text:`I always ___ my sister from school at four o'clock.`,answers:[`pick up`]},{id:`b1-wb-016`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Traffic lights' are the red, yellow and green lights at a road junction. Example: 'Stop at the traffic lights.'`,wrongReasons:{jams:`A 'traffic jam' is a long line of cars that cannot move. It is not a fixed place where you turn.`,lamps:`'lamps' are lights in a house. In the street we say 'traffic lights'.`,colours:`'traffic colours' is not an English expression.`},bank:[`lights`,`lamps`,`jams`,`colours`],text:`Turn left at the traffic ___ and the bank is on your right.`,answers:[`lights`]},{id:`b1-wb-017`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`In a relative clause with commas (extra information), use 'who' for people. 'That' is not possible with commas. Example: 'My aunt, who lives in Leeds, is a teacher.'`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that' cannot be used in a relative clause between commas. Use 'who' for people here.`,which:`'which' is for things. Your grandfather is a person.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun, for example 'whose wife'.`},bank:[`whose`,`which`,`who`,`that`],text:`My grandfather, ___ is 90, still rides his bike every day.`,answers:[`who`]},{id:`b1-wb-018`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'good at' (adjective + preposition) we use the -ing form. Example: 'He is bad at saying no.'`,wrongReasons:{"to remember":`After the preposition 'at' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,remember:`The base form cannot follow 'at'. Use the -ing form.`,remembered:`'remembered' is past. After 'at' we need '-ing'.`},bank:[`remembered`,`remember`,`remembering`,`to remember`],text:`My sister is very good at ___ people's names.`,answers:[`remembering`]},{id:`b1-wb-019`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: present simple in both parts for general facts. Example: 'Ice melts if it isn't cold.'`,wrongReasons:{"won't":`'won't' is future. General facts use the present simple in both parts.`,"didn't":`'didn't' is past. This is a general fact, so we use the present.`,"aren't":`'aren't' is the negative of 'be'. With the main verb 'get' we need 'don't'.`},bank:[`didn't`,`aren't`,`don't`,`won't`],text:`Plants die if they ___ get any water.`,answers:[`don't`]},{id:`b1-wb-020`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Turn down' means make quieter. Example: 'Please turn down the TV.'`,wrongReasons:{"turn up":`'turn up' means make louder. The music is already too loud.`,"look after":`'look after' means take care of someone or something, not change the volume.`,"give up":`'give up' means stop doing a habit or activity. It is not used for volume.`},bank:[`turn down`,`look after`,`give up`,`turn up`],text:`Can you ___ the music, please? It's too loud and I'm trying to sleep.`,answers:[`turn down`]},{id:`b1-wb-021`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'don't have to' for something that is not necessary but is allowed. Example: 'You don't have to wear a tie, but you can.'`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means it is forbidden, but the sentence says 'you can if you want'.`,"have to":`'have to' means it is necessary. 'but you can if you want' shows that it is your choice.`,"can't":`'can't' means it is not allowed or possible, which contradicts 'you can if you want'.`},bank:[`have to`,`mustn't`,`can't`,`don't have to`],text:`You ___ bring food to the party, but you can if you want.`,answers:[`don't have to`]},{id:`b1-wb-022`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'should' to give advice. Example: 'You should drink more water.'`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means it is forbidden. Going to bed earlier is good advice, not forbidden.`,"couldn't":`'couldn't' is past ability. The sentence gives advice for now.`,"can't":`'can't' means it is impossible. Being tired is a reason for advice, not for impossibility.`},bank:[`should`,`mustn't`,`can't`,`couldn't`],text:`You look tired. You ___ go to bed earlier.`,answers:[`should`]},{id:`b1-wb-023`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people and 'which' for things. Example: 'The teacher who helped me has a car which is red.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:which":`'which' is for things, but here we mean the girl, a person.`,"0:where":`'where' is for places, not for a person.`,"0:whose":`'whose' needs a noun after it, for example 'whose brother'.`,"1:who":`'who' is for people. A computer is a thing.`,"1:where":`'where' means 'in that place'. Here the computer is the subject of 'is very old'.`,"1:whose":`'whose' shows possession and cannot be followed directly by a verb like 'is'.`},bank:[`whose`,`who`,`where`,`which`],text:`The girl ___ sits next to me has a computer ___ is very old.`,answers:[`who`,`which`]},{id:`b1-wb-024`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people in relative clauses. Example: 'The man who works here is very kind.'`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' is used for things and animals, not for people.`,where:`'where' is used for places, not for people.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun, for example 'whose son'.`},bank:[`who`,`which`,`where`,`whose`],text:`The woman ___ lives next door is a nurse.`,answers:[`who`]},{id:`b1-wb-025`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'so that' + a clause to give a purpose. Example: 'I saved money so that I could travel.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast. Talking to your grandmother is the purpose, not a contrast.`,"because of":`'because of' must be followed by a noun, not by a full clause.`,unless:`'unless' means 'if not'. 'I want to learn Spanish if I can't talk' does not make sense.`},bank:[`so that`,`because of`,`although`,`unless`],text:`I want to learn Spanish ___ I can talk to my grandmother.`,answers:[`so that`]},{id:`b1-wb-026`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for ability in the past. Example: 'My grandmother could speak three languages.'`,wrongReasons:{can:`'can' is for the present. 'When I was five' is in the past, so we need 'could'.`,must:`'must' expresses obligation, not ability. The sentence compares things you were able to do.`,should:`'should' gives advice. Here we are talking about what you were able to do.`},bank:[`should`,`must`,`can`,`could`],text:`When I was five, I ___ swim, but I couldn't ride a bike.`,answers:[`could`]},{id:`b1-wb-027`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'that' (or 'which') for things in relative clauses. Example: 'Where is the book that I lent you?'`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who' is for people. Keys are things.`,where:`'where' is for places. Keys are not a place.`,what:`'what' means 'the thing that', so it cannot come after a noun like 'the keys'.`},bank:[`who`,`where`,`what`,`that`],text:`I've lost the keys ___ you gave me.`,answers:[`that`]},{id:`b1-wb-028`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'agree' we use 'to' + verb. Example: 'He agreed to help me.'`,wrongReasons:{finishing:`'agree' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,finish:`After 'agreed' we need 'to' before the verb.`,finished:`'finished' is past; after 'agreed' we need the infinitive with 'to'.`},bank:[`finish`,`to finish`,`finished`,`finishing`],text:`The two companies agreed ___ the project early next year.`,answers:[`to finish`]},{id:`b1-wb-029`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Find out' means discover information. Example: 'I found out that the shop closes at six.'`,wrongReasons:{"looked after":`'looked after' means took care of. You learn facts; you don't take care of them.`,"gave up":`'gave up' means stopped. It does not fit with learning facts.`,"turned off":`'turned off' is used for machines and lights, not information.`},bank:[`gave up`,`turned off`,`looked after`,`found out`],text:`While I was reading this book, I ___ a lot of interesting facts about whales.`,answers:[`found out`]},{id:`b1-wb-030`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'However' starts a new sentence to show a contrast and is followed by a comma. Example: 'The car is old. However, it works well.'`,wrongReasons:{because:`'because' needs a clause after it, not a comma, and the noise is not a reason for liking the city.`,so:`'so' shows a result. The noise is not a result of liking the city.`,although:`'although' must be followed directly by a clause in the same sentence; it is not used with a comma like this.`},bank:[`because`,`so`,`although`,`however`],text:`I really like living in the city. ___, it is too noisy at night.`,answers:[`however`]},{id:`b1-wb-031`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Take off' means remove clothes or shoes. Example: 'Take off your jacket and sit down.'`,wrongReasons:{"put on":`'put on' means start wearing. Wet shoes would make the clean floor dirty.`,"turn on":`'turn on' is used for machines and lights, not shoes.`,"get up":`'get up' means leave your bed. It does not take an object like 'shoes'.`},bank:[`turn on`,`get up`,`put on`,`take off`],text:`Please ___ your wet shoes. I've just cleaned the floor.`,answers:[`take off`]},{id:`b1-wb-032`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'that' for things. The preposition can stay at the end. Example: 'This is the film that I told you about.'`,wrongReasons:{whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`,where:`'where' is for places. You talk about a book; it is not a place.`,who:`'who' is for people, but a book is a thing.`},bank:[`where`,`whose`,`who`,`that`],text:`Is this the book ___ you were talking about?`,answers:[`that`]},{id:`b1-wb-033`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After a noun like 'the thing', use 'that' or 'which', not 'what'. Example: 'The thing that I like most is the beach.'`,wrongReasons:{what:`'what' already means 'the thing that'. 'The thing what' repeats the idea and is wrong.`,who:`'who' is for people, but 'the thing' is not a person.`,where:`'where' is for places. The sentence talks about something you need, not a place.`},bank:[`that`,`where`,`who`,`what`],text:`The thing ___ I need most right now is a holiday.`,answers:[`that`]},{id:`b1-wb-034`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Cheap' means it does not cost much money. Example: 'The bus is cheaper than the train.'`,wrongReasons:{expensive:`'expensive' means it costs a lot. Ten pounds is a low price.`,rich:`'rich' describes people with a lot of money, not prices.`,poor:`'poor' describes people with little money or bad quality, not a low price.`},bank:[`cheap`,`expensive`,`poor`,`rich`],text:`This jacket was very ___. It only cost ten pounds.`,answers:[`cheap`]},{id:`b1-wb-035`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Lend' = give something for a short time; 'borrow' = take it for a short time. Example: 'Can you lend me some money?'`,wrongReasons:{borrow:`'borrow' means take from someone. The structure is 'Can I borrow your pen?', not 'borrow me'.`,rent:`'rent' means pay money to use something like a car or a flat, not a pen for a minute.`,"give back":`'give back' means return something. You cannot return a pen you never had.`},bank:[`borrow`,`rent`,`lend`,`give back`],text:`Could you ___ me your pen for a minute? I've left mine at home.`,answers:[`lend`]},{id:`b1-wb-036`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Give up' means stop doing a habit. Example: 'She gave up chocolate for a month.'`,wrongReasons:{"gave back":`'gave back' means returned something to someone. You cannot return smoking.`,"looked after":`'looked after' means took care of. It is not used with a habit like smoking.`,"found out":`'found out' means discovered information. It is not about stopping a habit.`},bank:[`gave up`,`looked after`,`gave back`,`found out`],text:`My dad ___ smoking ten years ago.`,answers:[`gave up`]},{id:`b1-wb-037`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for how things work: if + present simple, present simple. Example: 'If you turn the key, the engine starts.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:stops":`'stops' does not make sense after 'you'. You do something TO the button: you press it.`,"0:will press":`Don't use 'will' in the 'if' part of a conditional.`,"0:stopped":`'stopped' is past and you cannot 'stop' a button. We need a present verb that means push.`,"1:press":`'press' does not fit 'the machine', and a singular subject needs -s in the present.`,"1:will press":`'will press' has the wrong meaning: the machine does not press anything.`,"1:stopped":`'stopped' is past. Instructions about how a machine works use the present simple.`},bank:[`will press`,`press`,`stops`,`stopped`],text:`If you ___ the red button, the machine ___.`,answers:[`press`,`stops`]},{id:`b1-wb-038`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base verb. Example: 'If you train every day, you will get faster.'`,wrongReasons:{would:`'would' is for imaginary situations (second conditional). With 'study' in the present, we use 'will'.`,did:`'did' is a past auxiliary. The result is in the future.`,are:`'are' cannot go before a base verb like 'pass'. 'you are pass' is wrong.`},bank:[`did`,`are`,`will`,`would`],text:`If you study hard, you ___ pass the exam.`,answers:[`will`]},{id:`b1-wb-039`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'mustn't' when something is forbidden. Example: 'You mustn't use your phone in the exam.'`,wrongReasons:{"don't have to":`'don't have to' means it is not necessary but it is allowed. Smoking here is not allowed.`,might:`'might' talks about possibility. The sentence is about a rule, not a possibility.`,should:`'should' gives advice to do something. Smoking is not allowed, so we cannot advise it.`},bank:[`should`,`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`might`],text:`You ___ smoke in the hospital. It is not allowed.`,answers:[`mustn't`]},{id:`b1-wb-040`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'which' for things. Example: 'The shoes which I bought are too small.'`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who' is only for people. A phone is a thing.`,where:`'where' is for places. You bought the phone; you did not buy something in it.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it, for example 'whose screen'.`},bank:[`where`,`who`,`whose`,`which`],text:`The phone ___ I bought last week has already stopped working.`,answers:[`which`]},{id:`b1-wb-041`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'decide' we use 'to' + verb. Example: 'We decided to stay at home.'`,wrongReasons:{buying:`'decide' is followed by 'to' + verb, not by the -ing form.`,buy:`After 'decided' we need 'to' before the verb.`,bought:`'bought' is past simple. We already have the past in 'decided'.`},bank:[`bought`,`to buy`,`buy`,`buying`],text:`After a long discussion, she decided ___ a new car.`,answers:[`to buy`]},{id:`b1-wb-042`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Get up' means leave your bed; 'put on' means start wearing. Example: 'I get up, wash and put on my uniform.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:put on":`'put on' needs an object (clothes) and describes dressing, not the start of the day.`,"0:take off":`'take off' needs an object, and planes take off; it does not mean leave your bed.`,"0:give up":`'give up' means stop doing something. It is not part of a morning routine here.`,"1:get up":`'get up' does not take 'my clothes' as an object.`,"1:take off":`After a shower you get dressed, so you don't remove your clothes.`,"1:give up":`'give up my clothes' has no clear meaning; 'give up' is for habits.`},bank:[`put on`,`get up`,`give up`,`take off`],text:`I ___ at seven, have a shower and then ___ my clothes.`,answers:[`get up`,`put on`]},{id:`b1-wb-043`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'might' to say something is possible in the future. Example: 'Take your keys. I might be out when you get home.'`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' is for things that are forbidden. The weather cannot be forbidden.`,"has to":`'has to' expresses obligation. The weather has no obligations.`,"can't":`'can't' means it is impossible. Then there would be no reason to take a coat.`},bank:[`might`,`mustn't`,`can't`,`has to`],text:`Take a coat. It ___ be cold later.`,answers:[`might`]},{id:`b1-wb-044`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'But' shows a contrast and 'so' shows a result. Example: 'I wanted to go, but I was busy, so I stayed at home.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:so":`'so' would mean the high price is a result of wanting the jacket, which is not logical.`,"0:because":`'because' gives a reason. The price is not the reason you wanted the jacket.`,"0:unless":`'unless' means 'if not'. It does not join a past wish and a past fact.`,"1:but":`'but' needs a contrast. Not buying an expensive jacket is a result, not a contrast.`,"1:because":`'because' gives a reason. 'I didn't' is the result of the price, not the reason for it.`,"1:unless":`'unless' introduces a condition; 'unless I didn't' has no clear meaning here.`},bank:[`because`,`but`,`unless`,`so`],text:`I wanted to buy the jacket, ___ it was too expensive, ___ I didn't.`,answers:[`but`,`so`]},{id:`b1-wb-045`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'might' for a possible plan when you are not sure. Example: 'I might go to the cinema tonight.'`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must' shows strong obligation or certainty, but you say 'I'm not sure yet'.`,"mustn't":`'mustn't' means something is forbidden. Visiting your aunt is not forbidden.`,"can't":`'can't' shows you are sure it is impossible, but you are not sure.`},bank:[`must`,`might`,`can't`,`mustn't`],text:`I'm not sure yet, but I ___ visit my aunt this weekend.`,answers:[`might`]},{id:`b1-wb-046`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'because of' + a noun to give a reason. Example: 'We stayed in because of the snow.'`,wrongReasons:{because:`'because' must be followed by a subject and a verb, for example 'because there was a storm'. Here we only have a noun.`,although:`'although' shows a contrast and needs a full clause. The storm is the reason, not a contrast.`,so:`'so' introduces a result with a full clause. The storm is the cause, not the result.`},bank:[`although`,`so`,`because of`,`because`],text:`The flight was cancelled ___ the storm.`,answers:[`because of`]},{id:`b1-wb-047`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Look after' means take care of. Example: 'Can you look after my cat this weekend?'`,wrongReasons:{"look for":`'look for' means try to find. Your brother is not lost.`,"give up":`'give up' means stop doing something. You cannot 'give up' your brother here.`,"put on":`'put on' is used for clothes, not for taking care of a person.`},bank:[`give up`,`put on`,`look after`,`look for`],text:`I need to ___ my little brother tonight while my parents are out.`,answers:[`look after`]},{id:`b1-wb-048`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'enjoy' we use the -ing form. Example: 'He enjoys cooking for his friends.'`,wrongReasons:{"to swim":`'enjoy' is never followed by 'to' + verb. Use the -ing form.`,swim:`After 'enjoy' the base form is wrong. We need 'swimming'.`,swam:`'swam' is past simple. A verb after 'enjoy' takes the -ing form.`},bank:[`to swim`,`swimming`,`swam`,`swim`],text:`I really enjoy ___ in the sea in summer.`,answers:[`swimming`]},{id:`b1-wb-049`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not'. Example: 'You won't get better unless you rest.' = 'if you don't rest'.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'if we leave right now' would make us catch the bus, not miss it. The meaning is wrong.`,when:`'when we leave right now' is not logical: leaving now is the way to catch the bus.`,because:`'because' gives a reason. Leaving now is not a reason for missing the bus.`},bank:[`because`,`when`,`if`,`unless`],text:`We will miss the bus ___ we leave right now.`,answers:[`unless`]},{id:`b1-wb-050`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Put on' means start wearing clothes. Example: 'Put on your hat, it's snowing.'`,wrongReasons:{"take off":`'take off' means remove clothes. When it's cold, you need your coat.`,"give up":`'give up' means stop doing something, not wear something.`,"look after":`'look after' means take care of. It does not mean wear.`},bank:[`look after`,`take off`,`put on`,`give up`],text:`It's cold outside, so ___ your coat before you go.`,answers:[`put on`]},{id:`b1-wb-051`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A 'weather forecast' tells you what the weather will be like. Example: 'The forecast says it will snow.'`,wrongReasons:{climate:`'climate' is the usual weather of a place over many years, not the next few days.`,temperature:`'weather temperature' is not natural, and temperature is only one part of the weather.`,season:`A 'season' is spring, summer, autumn or winter. 'weather season' does not make sense.`},bank:[`forecast`,`climate`,`temperature`,`season`],text:`Let's check the weather ___ for the weekend before we plan the trip.`,answers:[`forecast`]},{id:`b1-wb-052`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for a place where something happened. Example: 'This is the park where I learned to ride a bike.'`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' would need 'at' ('at which'), because they had their date AT the restaurant.`,who:`'who' is for people. A restaurant is a place.`,what:`'what' cannot come after a noun like 'the restaurant'.`},bank:[`which`,`what`,`where`,`who`],text:`This is the restaurant ___ my parents had their first date.`,answers:[`where`]},{id:`b1-wb-053`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'want' + person + 'to' + verb. Example: 'My boss wants me to work on Saturday.'`,wrongReasons:{studying:`After 'want me' we use 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,study:`'want me study' is missing 'to'.`,studied:`'studied' is past. The structure is 'want someone to do something'.`},bank:[`study`,`studying`,`studied`,`to study`],text:`My parents want me ___ medicine at university.`,answers:[`to study`]},{id:`b1-wb-054`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Don't have to' means it is not necessary; 'have to' means it is necessary. Example: 'You don't have to book, but you have to arrive early.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:have to":`'have to' in the first part would remove the contrast with 'but ... the special exhibition'.`,"0:mustn't":`'mustn't' means paying is forbidden. Paying is not forbidden; it is just not necessary.`,"0:can't":`'can't pay' means you are not able to pay, which is not the idea here.`,"1:don't have to":`'don't have to' again would mean there is no contrast, so 'but' would not make sense.`,"1:mustn't":`'mustn't' would mean paying for the exhibition is forbidden, which is not logical for a museum.`,"1:can't":`'can't pay' talks about being unable. The sentence explains what is necessary.`},bank:[`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`can't`,`have to`],text:`You ___ pay to enter the museum, but you ___ pay for the special exhibition.`,answers:[`don't have to`,`have to`]},{id:`b1-wb-055`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Look for' means try to find. Example: 'I'm looking for my phone.'`,wrongReasons:{"looking after":`'looking after' means taking care of. You are trying to find your glasses.`,"finding out":`'finding out' is used for information or facts, not for objects you have lost.`,"giving up":`'giving up' means stopping. It does not match 'Have you seen them?'.`},bank:[`looking after`,`looking for`,`finding out`,`giving up`],text:`I'm ___ my glasses. Have you seen them anywhere?`,answers:[`looking for`]},{id:`b1-wb-056`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`For planes, 'take off' means leave the ground. Example: 'Our flight took off on time.'`,wrongReasons:{"took on":`'took on' means accepted work or employed someone. It is not used for planes leaving.`,"got up":`'got up' is for people leaving their bed, not for planes.`,"put off":`'put off' means delayed. It does not fit 'and landed two hours later'.`},bank:[`took off`,`took on`,`got up`,`put off`],text:`The plane ___ at 6 a.m. and landed in Rome two hours later.`,answers:[`took off`]},{id:`b1-wb-057`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Get on (well) with someone' means have a good relationship. Example: 'I get on well with my colleagues.'`,wrongReasons:{"get up":`'get up' means leave your bed. It is not about relationships.`,"look after":`'look after' means take care of. 'look after well with' is not correct.`,"turn down":`'turn down' means refuse or make quieter. It is not followed by 'well with'.`},bank:[`get up`,`turn down`,`look after`,`get on`],text:`I don't ___ well with my neighbour. We often argue.`,answers:[`get on`]},{id:`b1-wb-058`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'book' a table, a room or a ticket to reserve it. Example: 'I booked a room at the hotel.'`,wrongReasons:{keep:`'keep' means continue to have something. You can't keep a table you don't have yet.`,order:`We 'order' food or drinks, not a table.`,buy:`You don't buy a table in a restaurant; you reserve it.`},bank:[`keep`,`book`,`order`,`buy`],text:`I'd like to ___ a table for two for Saturday evening.`,answers:[`book`]},{id:`b1-wb-059`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: 'If she calls, I will tell her.'`,wrongReasons:{"will rain":`We don't use 'will' in the 'if' part of a first conditional, even about the future.`,rained:`'rained' is past. With 'will' in the main clause, the 'if' part uses the present simple.`,raining:`'raining' is not a full verb here. You would need 'is raining', and the simple form is better.`},bank:[`rained`,`will rain`,`rains`,`raining`],text:`If it ___ tomorrow, we will stay at home.`,answers:[`rains`]},{id:`b1-wb-060`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Unless' + present simple, will + verb. Example: 'Unless you study, you will fail.'`,wrongReasons:{"would be":`'would be' is used for imaginary situations. 'Unless you hurry' is a real possibility.`,were:`'were' is past. Being late is a future result.`,been:`'been' is a past participle and needs 'have' before it.`},bank:[`been`,`would be`,`will be`,`were`],text:`Unless you hurry, you ___ late for school.`,answers:[`will be`]},{id:`b1-wb-061`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'had to' for obligation in the past. 'Must' has no past form. Example: 'We had to wait for an hour.'`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must' has no past form. For the past ('yesterday') we use 'had to'.`,"have to":`'have to' is present. The sentence talks about yesterday.`,should:`'should' gives advice about the present or future, not a past obligation.`},bank:[`have to`,`had to`,`must`,`should`],text:`I ___ go to the dentist yesterday because I had toothache.`,answers:[`had to`]},{id:`b1-wb-062`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'to' + verb to explain why someone does something (purpose). Example: 'I went to the library to study.'`,wrongReasons:{"for buying":`'for' + -ing describes what a thing is used for. To explain a person's reason, use 'to' + verb.`,buying:`The -ing form alone does not show purpose here.`,buy:`The base form without 'to' cannot show purpose after 'went to the baker's'.`},bank:[`buy`,`buying`,`to buy`,`for buying`],text:`He went to the baker's ___ some fresh bread.`,answers:[`to buy`]},{id:`b1-wb-063`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'must' when you are almost sure something is true because of the evidence. Example: 'He's yawning. He must be tired.'`,wrongReasons:{"can't":`'can't' means you are sure it is NOT true. The car and the lights show the opposite.`,"might not":`'might not' shows doubt. The evidence makes us quite sure that she is at home.`,"doesn't have to":`'doesn't have to' is about obligation, not about how sure we are.`},bank:[`might not`,`can't`,`doesn't have to`,`must`],text:`She ___ be at home. Her car is outside and the lights are on.`,answers:[`must`]},{id:`b1-wb-064`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We say 'make a decision'. Example: 'It's hard to make a decision when you're tired.'`,wrongReasons:{do:`'do' goes with tasks and work, like homework, but not with 'decision'.`,get:`'get a decision' is not the usual way to say you decide something yourself.`,say:`'say' is used for words, not decisions. We say 'make a decision'.`},bank:[`make`,`say`,`get`,`do`],text:`I need to ___ a decision about my new job by Friday.`,answers:[`make`]},{id:`b1-wb-065`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'could you...?' to make a polite request. Example: 'Could you open the window, please?'`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must you...?' asks about obligation and sounds rude. It is not a polite request.`,should:`'should you...?' asks for advice or about duty, not for a favour.`,have:`'have' is not a modal verb for requests, and 'have you help me' is not correct.`},bank:[`must`,`have`,`could`,`should`],text:`Excuse me, ___ you help me with my bag, please?`,answers:[`could`]},{id:`b1-wb-066`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'which' when the relative pronoun is the object of the verb. You visit a city, so 'which' is the object. Example: 'Rome is a city which I love.'`,wrongReasons:{where:`'where' means 'in that place'. The verb 'visit' needs an object (you visit the city), so we need 'which', not 'where'.`,who:`'who' is for people. A city is a place.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun.`},bank:[`where`,`who`,`whose`,`which`],text:`Paris is a city ___ millions of tourists visit every year.`,answers:[`which`]},{id:`b1-wb-067`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Delayed' means later than planned. Example: 'The train was delayed by twenty minutes.'`,wrongReasons:{lately:`'lately' means recently. It is an adverb and does not describe the flight.`,slowly:`'slowly' is an adverb of speed. 'was slowly because of the fog' is not correct.`,missed:`People miss a flight when they are late. Fog does not make a flight 'missed'.`},bank:[`slowly`,`delayed`,`missed`,`lately`],text:`Our flight was ___ because of the fog, so we waited at the airport for three hours.`,answers:[`delayed`]},{id:`b1-wb-068`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'can't' when you are sure something is impossible. Example: 'You've just eaten. You can't be hungry.'`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must' means you are sure it IS true. But John is in Spain, so it is not him.`,should:`'should' means you expect something. We don't expect John because he is away.`,"has to":`'has to' is about obligation. Here we are guessing who is at the door.`},bank:[`should`,`has to`,`can't`,`must`],text:`That ___ be John at the door. He's on holiday in Spain.`,answers:[`can't`]},{id:`b1-wb-069`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'whose' to show possession: 'the boy whose bike' = 'the boy's bike'. Example: 'She's the girl whose dog bit me.'`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who' cannot be followed directly by a noun like 'bike'. We need a word that means 'his'.`,which:`'which' is for things and cannot show that the bike belongs to the boy.`,that:`'that' cannot show possession. 'the boy that bike' is not correct English.`},bank:[`which`,`whose`,`that`,`who`],text:`That's the boy ___ bike was stolen.`,answers:[`whose`]},{id:`b1-wb-070`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'in case' to say you do something because something might happen. Example: 'Bring some water in case you get thirsty.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast. Taking an umbrella is not in contrast with rain.`,so:`'so' introduces a result. Rain later is not the result of taking an umbrella.`,however:`'however' is an adverb that starts a new sentence. It cannot join two parts of one sentence.`},bank:[`in case`,`however`,`although`,`so`],text:`Take an umbrella ___ it rains later.`,answers:[`in case`]},{id:`b1-wb-071`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After a preposition like 'in', 'at' or 'of', use the -ing form. Example: 'She's good at drawing.'`,wrongReasons:{"to learn":`After the preposition 'in' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,learn:`After a preposition the base form is wrong. Use the -ing form.`,learnt:`'learnt' is past. After 'in' we need the -ing form.`},bank:[`learn`,`learnt`,`learning`,`to learn`],text:`My cousin is very interested in ___ photography.`,answers:[`learning`]},{id:`b1-wb-072`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'finish' we use the -ing form. Example: 'I finished reading the book last night.'`,wrongReasons:{"to write":`'finish' is followed by the -ing form, not by 'to' + verb.`,write:`After 'finished' the base form is not correct.`,wrote:`'wrote' is past simple. After 'finished' we need '-ing'.`},bank:[`writing`,`to write`,`write`,`wrote`],text:`Have you finished ___ the report for the meeting?`,answers:[`writing`]},{id:`b1-wb-073`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'promise' + to + verb; 'keep' + -ing form. Example: 'He promised to call, but he keeps forgetting.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:forgetting":`'promised forgetting' is wrong: 'promise' takes 'to' + verb, and the meaning is not logical.`,"0:cleaning":`'promise' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,"0:to forget":`'promised to forget my room' has no sensible meaning.`,"1:to clean":`'keep' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,"1:cleaning":`'keep cleaning about it' does not make sense; you forget ABOUT something.`,"1:to forget":`'keep' needs the -ing form after it, so 'keep to forget' is wrong.`},bank:[`to forget`,`to clean`,`cleaning`,`forgetting`],text:`I promised ___ my room, but I keep ___ about it.`,answers:[`to clean`,`forgetting`]},{id:`b1-wb-074`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'pass' a test or exam. Example: 'She passed her English exam with a high mark.'`,wrongReasons:{win:`We 'win' a game or a prize, not a test.`,succeed:`'succeed' needs 'in': 'succeed in a test'. Without 'in' it is wrong.`,earn:`We 'earn' money or respect, not a test.`},bank:[`succeed`,`earn`,`win`,`pass`],text:`I hope I ___ my driving test tomorrow.`,answers:[`pass`]},{id:`b1-wb-075`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'must' for strong obligation, for example rules and laws. Example: 'You must stop at a red light.'`,wrongReasons:{might:`'might' expresses possibility. A law is not just a possibility; it is an obligation.`,could:`'could' expresses ability or a possibility. It is too weak for a law.`,"shouldn't":`'shouldn't' means it is a bad idea. The law says you need to wear a seatbelt, so this is the opposite.`},bank:[`might`,`could`,`shouldn't`,`must`],text:`You ___ wear a seatbelt in the car. It's the law.`,answers:[`must`]},{id:`b1-wb-076`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places when it means 'in/at that place'. Example: 'That's the café where we met.'`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who' is used for people, but a hotel is a place.`,which:`'which' needs a preposition here ('in which'), because we stayed IN the hotel. Without 'in', it is wrong.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},bank:[`which`,`where`,`whose`,`who`],text:`This is the hotel ___ we stayed last summer.`,answers:[`where`]},{id:`b1-wb-077`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'miss' a bus or train when we arrive too late. Example: 'Hurry up or we'll miss the train.'`,wrongReasons:{lost:`We 'lose' objects like keys. When you are too late for a bus, you 'miss' it.`,forgot:`'forgot' means you did not remember. It does not explain arriving too late for the bus.`,failed:`We 'fail' an exam or a test, not a bus.`},bank:[`failed`,`forgot`,`lost`,`missed`],text:`She ___ her bus this morning, so she was late for work.`,answers:[`missed`]},{id:`b1-wb-078`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'hope' we use 'to' + verb. Example: 'I hope to finish by six.'`,wrongReasons:{seeing:`'hope' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,see:`After 'hope' we need 'to' before the verb.`,saw:`'saw' is past. 'hope' talks about the future, and it needs 'to see'.`},bank:[`seeing`,`saw`,`see`,`to see`],text:`We hope ___ you again at the next family party.`,answers:[`to see`]},{id:`b1-wb-079`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + verb in the main clause, present simple after 'if'. Example: 'I will buy you a gift if I see one.'`,wrongReasons:{sent:`'sent' is past. Going to Rome is a future possibility.`,sending:`'sending' cannot be the main verb without 'am'.`,sends:`'sends' is for he/she/it, not for 'I', and the result is in the future.`},bank:[`sending`,`sends`,`will send`,`sent`],text:`I ___ you a postcard if I go to Rome.`,answers:[`will send`]},{id:`b1-wb-080`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We say 'do someone a favour'. Example: 'Could you do me a favour and close the door?'`,wrongReasons:{make:`We 'make' things like cakes or mistakes, but a favour goes with 'do'.`,take:`'take' does not go with 'favour'. The fixed expression is 'do me a favour'.`,have:`'have' a favour is not a natural English expression.`},bank:[`take`,`make`,`have`,`do`],text:`Can you ___ me a favour and carry this box?`,answers:[`do`]},{id:`b1-wb-081`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'avoid' we use the -ing form. Example: 'Try to avoid eating late at night.'`,wrongReasons:{"to talk":`'avoid' is never followed by 'to' + verb.`,talk:`After 'avoids' the base form is wrong; use the -ing form.`,talked:`'talked' is past. 'always avoids' shows a habit and needs '-ing' after it.`},bank:[`talk`,`to talk`,`talked`,`talking`],text:`She always avoids ___ about her problems at work.`,answers:[`talking`]},{id:`b1-wb-082`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'don't have to' when something is not necessary. Example: 'You don't have to come if you're busy.'`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means it is forbidden. Getting up early on Sunday is not forbidden, just not necessary.`,"can't":`'can't' means it is impossible. Sunday does not make getting up early impossible.`,should:`'should' gives advice. Sunday is not a good reason to get up early, so 'so ... should' is not logical.`},bank:[`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`should`,`can't`],text:`Tomorrow is Sunday, so I ___ get up early.`,answers:[`don't have to`]},{id:`b1-wb-083`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'as soon as' + present simple to say 'immediately when' in the future. Example: 'I'll text you as soon as I get home.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast. Arriving and calling are not in contrast.`,so:`'so' introduces a result. Arriving at the hotel is not the result of the phone call.`,"because of":`'because of' must be followed by a noun, not by a subject and a verb.`},bank:[`as soon as`,`because of`,`although`,`so`],text:`I'll call you ___ I arrive at the hotel.`,answers:[`as soon as`]},{id:`b1-wb-084`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for facts: if + present simple, present simple. Example: 'If you add salt to water, it boils at a higher temperature.'`,wrongReasons:{got:`'got' is past, but this is a fact that is always true.`,getting:`'getting' needs 'are' before it and is not a full verb here.`,gets:`With 'you', the present simple does not take -s.`},bank:[`get`,`gets`,`getting`,`got`],text:`If you mix red and yellow, you ___ orange.`,answers:[`get`]},{id:`b1-wb-085`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for habits and general truths: present simple in both parts. Example: 'My dog barks if someone knocks.'`,wrongReasons:{"will be":`In the 'if' part of a zero conditional we use the present simple, not 'will'.`,were:`'were' is past, but 'gets angry' shows a habit in the present.`,be:`'be' is the base form. After 'students' we need the present form 'are'.`},bank:[`were`,`are`,`be`,`will be`],text:`My teacher gets angry if students ___ late.`,answers:[`are`]},{id:`b1-wb-086`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'then' to show the next step in a sequence. Example: 'Wash the vegetables. Then, cut them.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast and must join two clauses. Here we are listing steps.`,because:`'because' introduces a reason. Adding milk is the next step, not a reason.`,"so that":`'so that' introduces a purpose inside one sentence; it does not start the next step of a recipe.`},bank:[`because`,`although`,`then`,`so that`],text:`First, mix the flour and the eggs. ___, add the milk slowly.`,answers:[`then`]},{id:`b1-wb-087`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'but' to join two contrasting ideas. Example: 'I like tea, but my wife likes coffee.'`,wrongReasons:{so:`'so' means the second part is a result of the first. Her love of art is not caused by her brother's maths.`,because:`'because' gives a reason. His maths skills are not the reason she prefers art.`,if:`'if' introduces a condition. These are two facts, not a condition and its result.`},bank:[`because`,`but`,`so`,`if`],text:`My brother is good at maths, ___ my sister prefers art.`,answers:[`but`]},{id:`b1-wb-088`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'where' to mean 'in that place'. Example: 'I grew up in a town where everybody knows each other.'`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' would need 'in' ('in which'). Without a preposition, 'which there are no cars' is wrong.`,who:`'who' is used for people. A village is a place.`,whose:`'whose' must be followed by a noun that belongs to someone.`},bank:[`who`,`where`,`whose`,`which`],text:`We stayed in a small village ___ there are no cars.`,answers:[`where`]},{id:`b1-wb-089`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Run out of' means have no more of something. Example: 'The car has run out of petrol.'`,wrongReasons:{"looked for":`'looked for' means tried to find. It does not explain why we need to buy milk.`,"given up":`'given up' means stopped having it as a habit, so you would not ask for more.`,"put on":`'put on' is used for clothes or weight, not for food you have finished.`},bank:[`run out of`,`put on`,`given up`,`looked for`],text:`We've ___ milk. Can you buy some on your way home?`,answers:[`run out of`]},{id:`b1-wb-090`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'so' to show a result. Example: 'It was sunny, so we ate outside.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a surprise. Passing after studying hard is an expected result, not a contrast.`,because:`'because' gives a reason. Passing the exams is not the reason she studied; it is the result.`,unless:`'unless' means 'if not' and introduces a condition, not a past result.`},bank:[`because`,`although`,`unless`,`so`],text:`She studied very hard, ___ she passed all her exams.`,answers:[`so`]},{id:`b1-wb-091`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A 'prescription' is a paper from a doctor that lets you get medicine. Example: 'Take this prescription to the chemist.'`,wrongReasons:{receipt:`A 'receipt' is the paper you get after you pay for something in a shop.`,recipe:`A 'recipe' tells you how to cook a dish.`,ticket:`A 'ticket' lets you travel or enter an event, not get medicine.`},bank:[`recipe`,`prescription`,`ticket`,`receipt`],text:`The doctor gave me a ___ for some medicine.`,answers:[`prescription`]},{id:`b1-wb-092`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: the 'if' part uses the present simple. Example: 'If I see him, I'll say hello.'`,wrongReasons:{"will find":`Don't use 'will' after 'if' in a first conditional. Use the present simple.`,found:`'found' is past and is used in the second conditional with 'would', not with 'I'll'.`,finding:`'finding' cannot be the main verb after 'I'. We need a present simple verb.`},bank:[`will find`,`found`,`find`,`finding`],text:`If I ___ your keys, I'll call you.`,answers:[`find`]},{id:`b1-wb-093`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'if' to give a condition. Example: 'You can go out if you finish your homework.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows a contrast. Finishing your vegetables is a condition, not a contrast.`,so:`'so' introduces a result. Finishing vegetables is not the result of having dessert.`,"because of":`'because of' must be followed by a noun, not by 'you finish'.`},bank:[`so`,`if`,`although`,`because of`],text:`You can have dessert ___ you finish your vegetables.`,answers:[`if`]},{id:`b1-wb-094`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Put off' means move to a later time. Example: 'They put off the match because of the rain.'`,wrongReasons:{"put on":`'put on' is used for clothes or for organising a show. It does not mean delay.`,"taken off":`'taken off' is used for clothes and planes, not for changing the date of a meeting.`,"found out":`'found out' means discovered. 'until next week' shows a change of time.`},bank:[`taken off`,`found out`,`put off`,`put on`],text:`Our meeting was ___ until next week because the manager was ill.`,answers:[`put off`]},{id:`b1-wb-095`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'despite' + noun or -ing form to show a contrast. Example: 'Despite feeling ill, she went to work.'`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' needs a subject and a verb, for example 'Although he has a lot of money'. It cannot be followed by '-ing' here.`,however:`'however' is not followed by an -ing form. It starts a new sentence and is followed by a comma.`,because:`'because' gives a reason and needs a full clause. Money is not the reason he is unhappy.`},bank:[`despite`,`however`,`although`,`because`],text:`___ having a lot of money, he is not happy.`,answers:[`despite`]},{id:`b1-wb-096`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Although' joins two clauses in one sentence; 'However,' starts a new sentence. Both show a contrast. Example: 'Although I was ill, I went out. However, I came home early.'`,wrongReasons:{"0:however":`'however' cannot introduce a clause that is joined to another clause. It needs its own sentence.`,"0:because":`'because' gives a reason. Bad weather is not a reason to go to the beach.`,"0:unless":`'unless' means 'if not' and talks about conditions, not about a past contrast.`,"1:although":`'although' must be followed by a clause, not by a comma and a new sentence.`,"1:because":`'Because,' with a comma cannot start a full sentence like this.`,"1:unless":`'unless' needs a condition after it; 'Unless, we did not swim' has no meaning.`},bank:[`although`,`because`,`unless`,`however`],text:`___ the weather was bad, we went to the beach. ___, we did not swim.`,answers:[`although`,`however`]},{id:`b1-wb-097`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'tell' + person + information. Example: 'Can you tell me the time?'`,wrongReasons:{say:`'say' is not followed directly by a person: we say something TO someone.`,speak:`'speak' is used for languages or talking in general, not 'speak me how'.`,talk:`'talk' needs 'to' or 'about' and cannot be followed by 'me how'.`},bank:[`talk`,`say`,`speak`,`tell`],text:`Can you ___ me how to get to the station?`,answers:[`tell`]},{id:`b1-wb-098`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'if' in a first conditional or an instruction, use the present simple. Example: 'Ask me if you have a question.'`,wrongReasons:{"will need":`We don't use 'will' after 'if' in this kind of sentence.`,needed:`'needed' is past. The sentence is about now or the future.`,needs:`With 'you' the present simple has no -s: 'you need'.`},bank:[`needs`,`needed`,`will need`,`need`],text:`Call me if you ___ any help.`,answers:[`need`]},{id:`b1-wb-099`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'who' as the subject of a relative clause about a person. Example: 'The girl who won the race is my cousin.'`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' refers to things, but here we are talking about a man.`,where:`'where' refers to places, not to a person.`,what:`'what' cannot follow a noun. After 'the man' we need a relative pronoun for people.`},bank:[`which`,`who`,`where`,`what`],text:`Do you know the name of the man ___ wrote this song?`,answers:[`who`]},{id:`b1-wb-100`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'should' to say something is a good idea. Example: 'We should leave now or we'll miss the bus.'`,wrongReasons:{"couldn't":`'couldn't' is past ability. The film starts soon, so we talk about now.`,"mustn't":`'mustn't' means hurrying is forbidden. With only five minutes, hurrying is a good idea.`,"didn't have to":`'didn't have to' talks about the past. The situation is happening now.`},bank:[`should`,`mustn't`,`couldn't`,`didn't have to`],text:`We ___ hurry. The film starts in five minutes.`,answers:[`should`]}]};export{e as default};